                    coach_r4_routine:       `<strong>That's resilience as a routine, not a rescue.</strong> A sensing cadence, a modular-capacity policy and a standing reserve rule mean the next discontinuity is cheaper than this one. Salwan's whole point: resilience must be deliberately built and re-built across the cycle, not improvised when the alarm sounds.`,
                    coach_r4_norisk:        `<strong>You handled the shock well and built nothing standing.</strong> No sensing cadence, no modularity policy, no reserve rule — the next discontinuity catches you exactly as flat-footed as this one did. Resilience is a routine, not a one-off save. <em>This is now a run-defining flag.</em>`,
                    coach_r4_pitch:         `<strong>That's the thesis a board chair trusts.</strong> You didn't claim to have predicted the shock — you built the adaptive capacity to survive whichever way it broke, and you're institutionalising the routine that makes the next one cheaper. Optionality, not prophecy.`,
                    coach_r4_roi:           `<strong>Pitching current-year ROI misses what the board actually scored.</strong> Margin this year says nothing about whether you survive the next discontinuity. The mandate was resilience by design — point to the optionality and the routine, not the quarter's profit.`,
                    coach_r4_luck:          `<strong>“We called the shock right” is the most dangerous story you can tell a board.</strong> You didn't forecast it — nobody could. Framing survival as superior prediction guarantees you under-build optionality next cycle, because you'll think you can see the future. You can't; that's the whole point.`,

