                    insight_resilient:      `Resilient by design: you climbed the dynamic-capability ladder — named and resourced the binding constraint, held a real optionality reserve, chose a reversible entry mode, and converted that pre-built slack into a 1–2 quarter pivot when the shock landed. You retained the contested share, the Resilience Index is in the top band, and you institutionalised the routine that makes the next shock cheaper. Optionality, not prediction.`,
                    insight_core:           `Core trap: you over-funded the declining ICE core. Margin looked brilliant for a quarter, then ICE orders collapsed faster than forecast and you faced the shock with no future capability and almost no slack. You optimised the engine while the road disappeared — a slow-motion failure the board's resilience mandate was written to prevent.`,
                    insight_allin:          `Conviction without slack: you committed almost the entire budget and held no meaningful reserve. Resilience isn't picking the right future — it's surviving the wrong one. The shock revived the platform you bet against and you had no optionality to flex back. Conviction is not agility.`,
                    insight_greenfield:     `Locked-in capital: you chose greenfield for maximum control and the shock hit mid-build, stranding sunk capital in a market whose demand assumption had just changed. Entry mode is an optionality decision, not only a control decision — a JV or license would have let you pivot at month nine instead of month thirty.`,
                    insight_starve:         `Starved the constraint: in Round 1 you correctly named embedded-software depth as the binding constraint, then in Round 2 you funded it with a token. When the seize moment came you couldn't bid the platform contract because you still lacked the engineers. Naming a constraint and resourcing it are not the same act.`,
                    insight_routine:        `Rescue, not routine: you handled the shock but built no standing capability — no sensing cadence, no modularity policy, no reserve rule. The next discontinuity will catch you exactly as flat-footed as this one. Salwan's point is that resilience is a routine that must be deliberately re-built across the cycle, not improvised when the alarm sounds.`,
                    insight_fastpivot:      `Fast pivot: your pre-built slack and capability converted into a 1–2 quarter redeployment when the shock landed — the reward path. Pivot speed was bought in advance, in Round 2, not in the moment.`,
                    insight_slowpivot:      `Slow pivot: with little slack and a thin capability build, your pivot took four-plus quarters and forfeited share that early optionality would have protected. Pivot feasibility is set before the shock, by the prior budget and slack decisions.`,
                    insight_sharelost:      `Share lost permanently: the late pivot forfeited 6–9 points of contested EV/SDV share that you will not recover. The cost of the slow pivot was decided rounds earlier, when the reserve was committed away.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed outcome: parts of the resilience playbook landed, but the constraint funding, the reserve, the entry mode and the pivot didn't fully reinforce each other — enough exposure that the board will question whether Aarav is genuinely resilient or merely lucky this time.`,

                    hint_core:              `You're over-defending the declining core — margin today, fragility at the shock. Redirect budget into the capability build and hold a real reserve before Round 3.`,
                    hint_allin:             `You've committed almost everything and kept no slack. The shock can revive the platform you bet against — preserve optionality, because conviction without a reserve is fragility.`,
                    hint_starve:            `You named the software gap but under-funded it. Resource the constraint to the level it needs (≈₹250 cr or the ₹380 cr acquisition), or you won't be able to seize the EV platform.`,
                    hint_greenfield:        `Greenfield locked your capital just as the shock changed the demand assumption. A reversible mode (JV or license) preserves the optionality to pivot — weigh control against agility.`,
